      "id": 1,
      "claim": "American federalism is the constitutional division of sovereignty between the national government and the states.",
      "evidence": "Political science definition and the U.S. Constitution’s allocation of powers to the federal government (Article I, §8) and to the states (10th Amendment).",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The constitutional provision that most directly expresses that division is the Tenth Amendment, which reserves to the states all powers not delegated to the United States.",
      "evidence": "Text of the Tenth Amendment: “The powers not delegated to the United States… are reserved to the States respectively, or to the people.” Legal scholarship cites it as the primary source of state sovereignty in federalism.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Option D is the Tenth Amendment, therefore it directly exemplifies American federalism.",
      "evidence": "Option D’s wording matches the amendment’s text and purpose.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Option A (checks and balances) concerns the separation of powers among the three branches of the federal government, not the national‑state power split.",
      "evidence": "Checks and balances allocate authority within the federal government (executive, legislative, judicial) per Articles I‑III, unrelated to federalism.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Option B (international treaty process) is an exclusive federal function and does not involve state authority.",
      "evidence": "Treaty-making power resides with the President and Senate (Article II, §2; Article I, §8), with no constitutional role for states.",
      "weight": 0.07,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Option C (special status of Washington, D.C.) deals with a federal district, not a principle of power sharing between federal and state governments.",
      "evidence": "Article I, §8, Clause 17 creates a district under congressional authority; it is a unique jurisdiction, not an illustration of federalism.",
      "weight": 0.08,
      "depends_on": [1]
    }
  ],
  "considered": [
